<p>When a hailstorm crosses McAllen, two very different professionals may end up looking at your roof. Understanding the <strong>public adjuster vs. insurance adjuster for hail claims in Texas</strong> question comes down to one thing: who each person works for. The insurance adjuster is sent by your carrier. A public adjuster is someone you hire. Both can be skilled and professional, but their job descriptions start from opposite sides of the table.</p>

<p>This guide explains how each role works on a hail claim, what Texas law says about public adjusters, how fees work, and how to decide whether your claim needs outside help. It is written for homeowners and business owners in McAllen, Hidalgo County, and the rest of the Rio Grande Valley (RGV).</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Public-Adjuster-vs.-Insurance-Adjuster-for-Hail-Claims-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster inspecting hail impacts on a composition shingle roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Both adjusters may inspect the same roof, but they represent different parties.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Two roles on the same claim</h2>

<h3>The insurance adjuster</h3>
<p>After you report a hail loss, your insurer assigns an adjuster. That person may be a staff employee of the carrier or an independent adjuster contracted for the storm, which is common after a large event when claim volume spikes. Their job is to investigate the loss, apply the policy as the carrier reads it, and write an estimate the insurer will use to make a payment decision.</p>
<p>Insurance adjusters follow the carrier’s guidelines and pricing, and they often have heavy caseloads after a regional storm. That is not a statement about anyone’s integrity. It simply means the inspection, the estimate, and the coverage position are prepared for the insurer.</p>

<h3>The public adjuster</h3>
<p>A public adjuster is licensed and regulated by TDI under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. You hire them by written contract, and that contract must state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.” A public adjuster typically reviews your policy, inspects and documents the damage, prepares an independent estimate, submits the claim package, and communicates with the carrier about the amount of the loss.</p>
<p>Texas also limits what public adjusters can do. They are not attorneys and cannot give legal advice, and under Chapter 4102 they cannot participate in the repair or reconstruction of property on a claim they are adjusting. That separation keeps the adjuster focused on the claim rather than on selling the repair.</p>

<h2>Key differences at a glance</h2>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Insurance adjuster</th><th>Public adjuster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who hires them</td><td>Your insurance company</td><td>You, the policyholder</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who pays them</td><td>The insurer (salary or per-claim fee)</td><td>You, usually a percentage of the settlement (capped at 10% in Texas)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Licensing</td><td>TDI adjuster license (or staff/emergency rules)</td><td>TDI public adjuster license, Chapter 4102</td></tr>
<tr><td>Primary task</td><td>Investigate and value the claim for the carrier</td><td>Document and present the claim for the insured</td></tr>
<tr><td>Makes the payment decision?</td><td>Recommends or decides for the carrier</td><td>No; presents evidence and negotiates the amount</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical starting point</td><td>Carrier guidelines and pricing</td><td>Your policy, the full damage scope, and local repair needs</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How each adjuster approaches a hail inspection</h2>

<p>A typical carrier inspection after a big storm is focused and time-boxed: confirm hail occurred, look for impact marks on the roof and soft metals, measure the roof, and write an estimate. On a straightforward claim, that may be all that is needed.</p>

<p>A public adjuster’s inspection is usually broader because the goal is a complete, defensible scope. On a typical McAllen home, that often means looking at:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Composition shingles</strong> for bruising, fractured mats, and granule loss at impact points on every slope, not only the most exposed one.</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals</strong> such as gutters, downspouts, roof vents, flashing, and AC condenser fins, which help show hail size and direction.</li>
<li><strong>Accessories</strong> like ridge vents, pipe jacks, drip edge, and starter course that must be replaced along with the roof covering.</li>
<li><strong>Exterior finishes</strong> common in the RGV, including stucco, painted trim, window screens, and metal roofs on outbuildings and carports.</li>
<li><strong>Interior and attic conditions</strong> where impacts have opened a leak path.</li>
<li><strong>Code items</strong> under the 2024 ICC codes the City of McAllen adopted effective January 1, 2026, which may affect what a compliant repair requires (see our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page).</li>
</ul>

<p>Neither approach guarantees a particular result. What a thorough inspection does is reduce the chance that real, covered damage is simply left out of the file.</p>

