<h2>Documentation and estimates</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Public-Adjuster-vs.-Insurance-Adjuster-for-Hail-Claims-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Organized hail claim file with roof photos, measurements, and estimate" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A well-organized file makes it easier for the carrier to review every item.</figcaption></figure>

<p>Most claims are decided on the file. The carrier’s adjuster documents what they saw to support the carrier’s estimate. A public adjuster builds the policyholder’s version of the file: labeled photo sets by slope and elevation, measurements, notes tying each item to the storm, a line-item estimate (commonly in Xactimate), and any supporting reports.</p>

<p>Our <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">guide to documenting hail damage</a> and the <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walk through what to gather yourself, whether or not you hire anyone.</p>

<h2>Supplements: correcting an incomplete scope</h2>

<p>A supplement is a request to add items that were missed, under-measured, or later discovered, such as additional slopes, drip edge, starter, ridge vents, pipe jacks, or damaged soft metals. Contractors submit supplements routinely, and carriers review them. The difference is mostly about who identifies the gaps and how well each item is supported. For a closer look, read <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements/">what carriers miss in their hail scope</a> or see our <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> service.</p>

<h2>Fees: what a public adjuster costs in Texas</h2>

<p>Public adjusters usually work on a percentage of the settlement. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. A few other rules are worth knowing:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Texas rule</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>10% fee cap</td><td>The adjuster’s percentage fee cannot exceed 10% of the insurance settlement on the claim.</td></tr>
<tr><td>72-hour policy-limits rule</td><td>If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the adjuster may not take a percentage fee (reasonable hourly compensation only).</td></tr>
<tr><td>TDI-approved contract</td><td>The contract must use approved terms and state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>72-hour rescission</td><td>You can cancel the contract within 72 hours of signing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>No repair work</td><td>Your public adjuster cannot also be your roofer or contractor on the same claim.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Whether hiring help makes financial sense depends on the size of the gap between the carrier’s estimate and the documented cost of a proper repair. If the estimate already matches your contractor’s scope, a fee may not be worth it. If large items are missing or the claim was denied, an independent review can clarify where you stand. A claim calculator can help you think through deductibles and depreciation.</p>

<h2>Policy language matters as much as damage</h2>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Public-Adjuster-vs.-Insurance-Adjuster-for-Hail-Claims-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowner reviewing insurance policy declarations page" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Your declarations page shows deductibles, limits, and roof settlement terms.</figcaption></figure>

<p>Two roofs with identical damage can be paid very differently because of the policy. Before comparing estimates, check your declarations page and endorsements for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductible.</strong> Many Texas policies use a separate percentage deductible based on your Coverage A (dwelling) limit.</li>
<li><strong>Roof settlement terms.</strong> Some policies pay replacement cost; others pay actual cash value or use a roof payment schedule based on age.</li>
<li><strong>Recoverable depreciation.</strong> Under replacement-cost policies, the insurer typically pays ACV first and releases the withheld depreciation after repairs are completed, often within a stated deadline.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions</strong> for metal roofing or other surfaces.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law coverage,</strong> which may pay for code-required upgrades up to its limit.</li>
</ul>

<p>Hidalgo County is not in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) area, so most McAllen properties have wind and hail coverage through their regular insurer (or the Texas FAIR Plan where applicable).</p>

<h2>When to consider hiring a public adjuster</h2>

<p>You can handle many hail claims on your own. Consider an independent review when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The claim was denied, or the carrier says the damage is “cosmetic” or “wear and tear.” See <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-mcallen/">what to do if your hail claim was denied</a>.</li>
<li>The estimate is well below your contractor’s scope and the carrier won’t explain the difference (<a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a>).</li>
<li>The loss includes interior damage, multiple structures, or a commercial roof.</li>
<li>Deadlines are slipping. Under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, insurers generally must acknowledge a claim within 15 days of notice and accept or reject within 15 business days after receiving requested items, with extensions allowed in some cases.</li>
<li>You simply don’t have time to manage inspections, documents, and follow-up.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to choose a public adjuster</h2>
<ul>
<li>Verify the license on the TDI website.</li>
<li>Read the contract: fee percentage, what it applies to, and the 72-hour rescission notice.</li>
<li>Ask how they document hail damage and prepare estimates.</li>
<li>Be cautious of anyone promising a specific settlement amount. No one can guarantee an outcome.</li>
<li>Confirm they are not also offering to do the repairs.</li>
</ul>

